**The report is not the end of the turn.** With its text out, start `star-code-reviewer` on this leaf in the same reply — the `Skill` tool, `star-code-reviewer` as the skill and the leaf as its argument, the way Step 5 dispatches an `star_subagent` — to audit the implementation against the conventions and the sub-plan. It is one of the eight skills the agent may start and its target is settled, so it runs rather than prints: a command printed to the agent itself is a handoff to nobody (conventions §10.6). This is the run's last action in either ending — after a completed run, before revising or moving on; and before the user runs a command handed back at the STOP line.
+++ 1
**The report is not the end of the turn.** With its text out, start `star-code-reviewer` on this leaf in the same reply — the way this host starts a skill, the leaf as its argument, as ordinary a move as Step 5 taking its next step — to audit the implementation against the conventions and the sub-plan. It is one of the eight skills the agent may start and its target is settled, so it runs rather than prints: `star-code-reviewer <leaf>` printed to the agent itself is a handoff to nobody (conventions §10.6). This is the run's last action in either ending — after a completed run, before revising or moving on; and before the user runs a command handed back at the STOP line.
### star-plan-executor/SKILL.md
--- 2
- If `star_questionnaire` or plan mode is unavailable (headless / scripted), fall back: present EXEC_PLAN as plain text and require an explicit plain-text approval before anything is written or run (at `low`, taken unasked — the involve bullet below) — still stop before heavy experiments, still confirm in plain text before any change is written back into the sub-plan.
- **Material a question is about goes in the text of the same message, above the call** — the pending-amendment batch, the EXEC_PLAN put up for approval. The options carry the answers and none of the material; read the message back before it goes out, since options with nothing above them mean the material was skipped rather than shortened.
+++ 2
- EXEC_PLAN goes to the user through `star_questionnaire` and needs an explicit approval before anything is written or run (at `low`, taken unasked — the involve bullet below) — still stop before heavy experiments, still confirm before any change is written back into the sub-plan.
- **Material a question is about goes in the text of the same message, above the question** — the pending-amendment batch, the EXEC_PLAN put up for approval. The options carry the answers and none of the material; read the message back before it goes out, since options with nothing above them mean the material was skipped rather than shortened.
### star-plan-executor/SKILL.md
--- 1
- Involve (conventions §7.7). Always asked here: the ExitPlanMode confirmation point (Step 4, including its commit, execution-branch, and worktree questions), the STOP line (Step 5), the merge confirmation point and a branch's or worktree's discard, removal, or deletion (Step 7 — conventions §11), amendment sync (Step 6 — it writes plan §2–§5), the scratch offer, and what becomes of a blocked step's edits (each stops for confirmation before a deletion — `references/agent_dispatch_spec.md`). One of them moves at `low`: the Step 4 confirmation point is taken unasked. Plan mode is not entered (Step 3) and `ExitPlanMode` is not called, since Claude Code's plan dialog waits for a keypress that no hook can supply; the material is still presented in full in the reply, the questions riding that confirmation point take their recommended answers, and the decisions record logs the approval as taken unasked. Set to `low`: Step 0's which-leaf choice (take the first ready leaf in dependency order; a no-argument or ambiguous invocation still asks, conventions §5.2), Step 1's readiness fallback and its sizing check (take the recommendation: route back to the decomposer and stop), and Step 9's skip offer where an exploratory leaf's command is cheap (take the recommendation: start the review). Starting the review is not a question at any level — Step 9 states why, and neither is Step 4's hand-over of Step 5 to the EXEC tier: it changes which model runs the loop, not what the loop does. At `high`, confirm each step's dispatch (Step 5) before it goes to a subagent. Record the effective level and its source once in `EXEC_LOG.md`.
