### star-refs-reviewer/SKILL.md
--- 1
Searching may fan out by query group, each given 2–3 of the queries, the search profile, its own share of the per-host request budget written as a number, and the same candidate format Step 4 uses — plus a capped verbatim `abstract` (~60 words) per candidate. That field is not optional: the main agent must present ~15 ranked candidates at this skill's only mandatory confirmation point, re-ranked against `refs_rubric.md`, whose core criterion — direct overlap with this method — is unjudgeable from title, venue, year and citation count. The re-rank is the main agent's: it drops any candidate it cannot justify in one clause. Each group also returns `failures: [{host, error, retries}]` and caches what it fetched under this run's own `raw/` prefix, one prefix per query group (conventions §6.4). Before the confirmation point, check rather than trust: per collector, re-open one cached page and compare two of its rows word for word against what was returned. Step 5 re-fetches every survivor's bibliographic record from scratch, but only after the confirmation point — what the user picks to read in depth is decided here, from lines nobody has re-opened.
+++ 1
Searching may fan out by query group, each dispatched to `star_subagent` (`agent: "star-collector"`) with 2–3 of the queries, the search profile, the same candidate format Step 4 uses, and its own share of the per-host request budget in `references/source_policy.md`, written as a number (conventions §6.9) — plus a capped verbatim `abstract` (~60 words) per candidate. That field is not optional: the main agent must present ~15 ranked candidates at this skill's only mandatory confirmation point, re-ranked against `refs_rubric.md`, whose core criterion — direct overlap with this method — is unjudgeable from title, venue, year and citation count. The re-rank is the main agent's: it drops any candidate it cannot justify in one clause. Each group also records `failures: [{host, error, retries}]` and caches what it fetched under this run's own `raw/` prefix, one prefix per query group (conventions §6.4). Before the confirmation point, check rather than trust: per group, re-open one cached page and compare two of its rows word for word against what the group's return says. Step 5 re-fetches every survivor's bibliographic record from scratch, but only after the confirmation point — what the user picks to read in depth is decided here, from lines nobody has re-opened.
### star-refs-reviewer/SKILL.md
--- 1
Rank candidates by the core-paper criteria in `references/refs_rubric.md` and present ~15 in one table, most relevant first. Each row also shows its provisional impact score — the citation and venue components only, computed by the rubric's arithmetic from the search records in hand and marked partial (`6.4*`): the code component waits for the paper pages at Step 3. The ranking stays relevance-first; the score is information beside the choice, not the sort key. Ask via one `star_questionnaire` over the table's numbered rows which to read deeply — ~15 rows cannot be options (conventions §7.3 caps a question at 4), so mark the 5–10 you recommend in the table and offer *read the recommended set* / *read a different set (say the numbers)* / *widen the search first*. The user may add papers of their own — fetch their records like any other.
+++ 1
Rank candidates by the core-paper criteria in `references/refs_rubric.md` and present ~15 in one table, most relevant first. Each row also shows its provisional impact score — the citation and venue components only, computed by the rubric's arithmetic from the search records in hand and marked partial (`6.4*`): the code component waits for the paper pages at Step 3. The ranking stays relevance-first; the score is information beside the choice, not the sort key. Ask via one `star_questionnaire` call over the table's numbered rows which to read deeply — ~15 rows cannot be options (conventions §7.3 caps a question at 4), so mark the 5–10 you recommend in the table and offer *read the recommended set* / *read a different set (say the numbers)* / *widen the search first*. The user may add papers of their own — fetch their records like any other.
### star-refs-reviewer/SKILL.md
--- 1
Reading may fan out to read-only `star_subagent` subagents (`subagent_type: Explore`), run in parallel, one paper each, each returning the note collector format in `references/refs_rubric.md` — not a filled template, which asks for fields only the writing session may set. Accept `depth: full` only where `depth_evidence` carries a real table caption and row; otherwise downgrade it. The main agent writes the files and owns §5 (Relation to This Project) — that section needs the method context and is the reason the note exists. Each collector gets its own share of the per-host request budget written as a number, and returns `failures: [{host, error, retries}]`; the pages it fetches are cached under this run's own `raw/` prefix, one prefix per paper (conventions §6.4). Steps 1 and 4 give their collectors the same three things — this step fetches from other hosts, not under other rules.
+++ 1
Reading goes one paper at a time — a read-only collection pass each, each filling the note collector format in `references/refs_rubric.md` — not a filled template, which asks for fields only the writing session may set. Accept `depth: full` only where `depth_evidence` carries a real table caption and row; otherwise downgrade it. The main agent writes the files and owns §5 (Relation to This Project) — that section needs the method context and is the reason the note exists. Requests go out one at a time against the whole per-host budget rather than split into shares, since nothing runs concurrently (conventions §6.9), and each pass records `failures: [{host, error, retries}]`; the pages it fetches are cached under this run's own `raw/` prefix, one prefix per paper (conventions §6.4). Steps 1 and 4 work the same way — this step fetches from other hosts, not under other rules.
### star-refs-reviewer/SKILL.md
--- 1
Expansion may fan out over the core papers, **one paper each** — how many collectors run at once (serial or concurrent) is the main agent's own call, with one hard bound: the request budget in `source_policy.md` is **per host**, not per agent, so a concurrent fan-out splits each host's budget across the collectors and writes each share into its brief as a number (conventions §6.9). Cite that file instead of repeating its numbers — they are kept current there. Each returns `candidates: [{title, first_author, year, venue, citation_count, external_ids, found_via, why: <one clause>}]`, `queries_run`, `failures: [{host, error, retries}]`, `papers_seen`, and nothing else. It fetches no bibliographic record and decides nothing about what is core; the pages it fetches are cached under this run's own `raw/` prefix, scoped to its own paper (conventions §6.4), and nothing else is written. Nothing it returns is trusted as a bib field — Step 5 re-fetches an authoritative record for every survivor from scratch, so a mis-transcribed title costs one failed match, routed to Needs-manual-check.
+++ 1
Expansion goes over the core papers, **one paper at a time** — which is also what the request budget in `source_policy.md` wants, since it is **per host** rather than per pass, and anything concurrent would multiply the real rate against every host this skill promises to be polite to. Cite that file instead of repeating its numbers — they are kept current there. Each returns `candidates: [{title, first_author, year, venue, citation_count, external_ids, found_via, why: <one clause>}]`, `queries_run`, `failures: [{host, error, retries}]`, `papers_seen`, and nothing else. It fetches no bibliographic record and decides nothing about what is core; the pages it fetches are cached under this run's own `raw/` prefix, scoped to its own paper (conventions §6.4), and nothing else is written. Nothing it returns is trusted as a bib field — Step 5 re-fetches an authoritative record for every survivor from scratch, so a mis-transcribed title costs one failed match, routed to Needs-manual-check.
### star-refs-reviewer/SKILL.md
